Experiment No. 2: Socket Programming for Transport Layer Packets (TCP Server)
Aim
Socket Programming for Transport Layer Packets (TCP Server).

Theory
A socket is a software abstraction representing an endpoint of a two-way communication link between two programs running on a network. It is uniquely identified by the combination of an IP Address and a Port Number.

At the Transport Layer of the OSI model, TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) provides connection-oriented, reliable, ordered, and error-checked data delivery. TCP Socket Programming on the server side operates as a Passive Open process, waiting for incoming connection requests from client nodes.

Server-Side Execution Steps:
Socket Creation (socket()): The server creates an endpoint using domain AF_INET (IPv4) and socket type SOCK_STREAM (TCP).
Binding (bind()): Binds the socket descriptor to a designated local IP address (INADDR_ANY) and port number (PORT = 10000 + 218 = 10218).
Listening (listen()): Transitions the socket into passive listening mode with a client backlog queue.
Accepting Connection (accept()): Blocks execution until an incoming connection arrives, completing the TCP Three-Way Handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) and returning a dedicated active socket descriptor (new_socket).
Data Transfer (read() / send()): Receives data buffers from client and echoes them back reliably.
Closing Connection (close()): Terminates client and listening sockets via the TCP four-wave termination sequence.
Extracted Source Code
File: server1218.c

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <netinet/in.h>

#define PORT (10000 + 218) // Port 10218
#define BUF_SIZE 1024

int main()
{
    int server_fd, new_socket;
    struct sockaddr_in address;
    char buffer[BUF_SIZE];
    int addrlen = sizeof(address);

    // Create TCP socket
    server_fd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    if (server_fd < 0) {
        perror("Socket creation failed");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Prepare server address
    address.sin_family = AF_INET;
    address.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;
    address.sin_port = htons(PORT);

    // Bind
    if (bind(server_fd, (struct sockaddr *)&address, sizeof(address)) < 0) {
        perror("Bind failed");
        close(server_fd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Listen
    if (listen(server_fd, 3) < 0) {
        perror("Listen failed");
        close(server_fd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    printf("TCP Echo Server listening on port %d...\n", PORT);

    // Accept client connection
    new_socket = accept(
        server_fd,
        (struct sockaddr *)&address,
        (socklen_t *)&addrlen
    );
    if (new_socket < 0) {
        perror("Accept failed");
        close(server_fd);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    while (1)
    {
        memset(buffer, 0, BUF_SIZE);

        int bytes = read(new_socket, buffer, BUF_SIZE);

        if (bytes <= 0)
            break;

        printf("Received: %s", buffer);

        // Echo back to client
        send(new_socket, buffer, bytes, 0);
    }

    close(new_socket);
    close(server_fd);

    return 0;
}
Conclusion
The TCP Server socket was successfully implemented at the Transport Layer. The passive open execution sequence (socket, bind, listen, and accept) was executed, establishing a reliable, connection-oriented channel capable of processing incoming client data streams.

How to Execute in Ubuntu Terminal
Step 1: Create the Source File
Open the file in gedit, paste the C code provided above, save and close:

gedit server1218.c
Step 2: Compile the Program
gcc server1218.c -o server
Step 3: Run the TCP Server (Terminal 1)
./server
Expected Output:

TCP Echo Server listening on port 10218...
Step 4: Test with a Client (Terminal 2)
In a second terminal window, connect via the TCP client or Netcat:

nc 127.0.0.1 10218
Observed Server Output:

Received: hello
Received: hiii
Received: test
Troubleshooting
If port 10218 is busy:

sudo fuser -k 10218/tcp
